This no longer seems to work with the new `nest()`, because the object is no longer a `List`.

I was able to get around this issue by using `nest_legacy()`, however, I would imagine that if `nest_legacy()` is eventually deprecated and dropped, this could be a problem.

I would advise that (1) people are made aware of this change, and (2) `nest_legacy()` is never deprecated / dropped.  If it were, that would mean that `process_nested_obj()` would have to be re-written.

There is not a good way to provide a variable name for a nested column in the new `nest()` function.

For example, the following code does not produces a data frame with a nested column named "another name". Also, this can't be fixed by using `rlang`'s curly brackets since it's on the left side of the parameter assignment.

```{r}
df <- tibble(x = c(1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3), y = 1:6, z = 6:1)

my_nest <- function(x, new_col_name, dots) { 
     x %>% nest(new_col_name = dots)
}

my_nest(df, "another name", c("y", "z"))
```

The only solution I've been able to come up with, which doesn't involve using deprecated functionality, is to write the expression `x %>% nest(new_col_name = dots)` as a string in the function, string substitute, parse, and eval. However, this seems like a step backwards from `nest()`'s previous incarnation.

