Per [this conversation](https://twitter.com/noamross/status/857609829480792064), it would be useful to have a function, or possibly an option in `drop_na()`, to drop rows from a data set that are _all_ NA, or which a range of columns are all NA (as opposed to one or more being NA).  Possible use cases include reading in excel sheets where rows are left blank as spacers for formatting purposes, or cases where some records have indexing values, but no actual data. 

@jennybc has a quick implementation here: https://gist.github.com/jennybc/a5c0710ec9d024cbd5c289a3d7569267

`janitor::drop_empty_rows()` works for the case where all the values are NA.

I'm not sure how common this use case is, but i find myself doing some version of this on almost every project.

```
drop_all_NA_rows <- function(df) {
  keep <- !apply(is.na(df), 1, all)
  df[keep, , drop = FALSE]
}

drop_all_NA_cols <- function(df, except = NULL) {
  maybe_drop <- !(names(df) %in% except)
  df[maybe_drop] <- lapply(df[maybe_drop], function(col)
    if (all(is.na(col))) NULL else col)
  df
}
```

This is usually done after reading in an excel file (typically with `readxl` or `openxlsx`) or a text file with `readr`. Most of the logs / tables / field forms I deal with, when read in, come with a bunch of empty rows and columns that are meaningless. 

This is particularly common with Excel files, or a text file that at some point in the recent past was an Excel files.

For datasets where a variable corresponds to a group and another to a time, a useful function would be to add rows for missing dates, making missing observation explicit.

``` R
mydata <- data_frame(
  name = c("john","john","john","john","mary","chris","chris","chris"),
  year = c(1999, 2000, 2001, 2003, 2001, 1998, 1999, 2003),
  score = rnorm(8)
)
```

`texpand(mydata, name, year)` is different from `expand(mydata, name, year)` in that it adds all dates between the min and the max of the last argument (2002 in the previous dataset)

This defines `texpand`:

``` R
#' @export
texpand <- function(data, ..., period = 1L) {
  dots <- lazyeval::lazy_dots(...)
  texpand_(data, dots, period = period)
}

#' @export
texpand_ <- function(data, dots, ..., period = 1L) {
  UseMethod("texpand_")
}

#' @export
texpand_.data.frame <- function(data, dots, ..., period = 1L) {
  dots <- lazyeval::as.lazy_dots(dots)
  if (length(dots) == 0)
    return(data.frame())
  iddots <- dots[-length(dots)]
  pieces <- lapply(iddots, unique_vals, data = data)
  timedot <- dots[length(dots)]
  time  <- dplyr::select_(data, .dots = timedot)[[1]]
  timev <- seq_time(time, period = period)
  check <- (time - timev[1]) %%  period
  if (sum(check)){
    stop("Time vector is not a regular sequence")
  }
  pieces[[length(pieces)+1]] <- data.frame(timev)
  Reduce(cross_df, pieces)
}
#' @export
texpand_.tbl_df <- function(data, dots, ..., period = period) {
  dplyr::tbl_df(NextMethod())
}

seq_time <- function(x, period) {
  attrs <- attributes(x)
  rng <- range(x, na.rm = TRUE)
  attributes(rng) <- attrs
  out <- seq(rng[1], rng[2], by = period)
}
```

Another direction than creating `texpand` and [`tlag`](https://github.com/hadley/dplyr/issues/1190) would be to define a new class of dataset, tbl_panel, which is a group + time. When setting the panel type, this checks that the time variable has no missing value and that there are no duplicate times by group. Then tidyr/dplyr has a special expand and a special lag for them.

When working on #133, I realized that separate() doesn't follow the usual convention, instead there is an "into" argument. The rationale seems to be that ... is used for extra args to strsplit().

I think the following interface would better suit the convention:

```
separate <- function(data, col, ..., sep = separator(), remove = TRUE,
                     convert = FALSE, extra = "warn", fill = "warn")
```

The ... args to strsplit() could be passed using a sentinel:

```
#' @export
separator <- function(sep = "[^[:alnum:]]+", ...) {
  separator_function <- function(value) {
    ...
  }
  structure(separator_function, class = "tidyr_separator")
}

as.tidyr_separator <- ... # UseMethod

as.tidyr_separator.character <- function(x) {
  separator(x)
}
```

