It can be useful to nest data frames so that contextual information is available inside the nests. Here's an  implementation sketch for a `nest_df()` function:

First a C++ util borrowed from purrr to turn a grouped data frame into a list of data frames:

``` C++
#include <Rcpp.h>
#include <dplyr.h>
using namespace Rcpp;

// [[Rcpp::export]]
SEXP extract_slices(const List data, const CharacterVector kept_cols) {
  ListOf<IntegerVector> indices(data.attr("indices"));
  int n_slices = indices.size();

  if (n_slices == 0) {
    return data[kept_cols];
  }

  CharacterVector classes = CharacterVector::create("tbl_df", "data.frame");
  dplyr::DataFrameSubsetVisitors visitors(data);

  List out = no_init(n_slices);
  for (int i = 0; i < n_slices; ++i) {
    List out_i = visitors.subset(indices[i], classes);
    out[i] = out_i[kept_cols];
  }

  return out;
}
```

The R functions:

``` r
nest_df <- function(data, key, ...) {
  nest_cols <- unname(dplyr::select_vars(names(data), ...))
  nest_df_(data, key, nest_cols)
}

nest_df_ <- function(data, key, nest_cols) {
  group_cols <- setdiff(names(data), nest_cols)

  dfs <- data %>%
    dplyr::group_by_(.dots = group_cols) %>%
    extract_slices(nest_cols)

  out <- data %>%
    dplyr::select_(.dots = group_cols) %>%
    dplyr::distinct()

  out[[key]] <- dfs
  dplyr::tbl_df(out)
}
```

